      {
        "ballot_id": "B001",
        "laureates": [
          "Charles L. Kane",
          "Eugene J. Mele",
          "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
        ],
        "citation": "For the theoretical prediction and experimental realization of the quantum spin Hall effect."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B017",
    "B030"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B001 as my preferred configuration. The quantum spin Hall effect connects a defined theoretical prediction to an experimental realization and opened a durable way to understand electronic phases through topology. Its testing in many systems over nearly two decades supports maturity, while a citation confined to this effect avoids claiming the whole topological-insulator field. B017 remains strong: its precision and experimental record are compelling, but the boundary between the foundational lattice-clock advances and the wider development of optical frequency standards still needs careful definition.",
    "case_against": "The experimental realization was a group effort, and crediting Molenkamp alone risks compressing that contribution. The boundaries between the initial quantum spin Hall work, later confirmations, and the wider topological-materials field also need historical scrutiny. These are serious attribution questions, though they do not displace the focused theory-to-experiment case.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors B017 for the mature clock achievement, arguing that Katori's lattice-trapping scheme and Ye's clock realization are complementary contributions that can be recognized with a narrow citation.",
        "response": "That is the strongest case for B017, and I agree that the two contributions give it a plausible focus. Maturity of the clocks is clear; the remaining question is how to separate the foundational advances from later gains and supporting standards work. B001 has a more concentrated discovery claim: a prediction and a landmark realization of one specified effect."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson favors B030 for the mature, widely used capability of sub-angstrom microscopy, while questioning whether aberration-corrected optics is distinct from prior Nobel-recognized electron microscopy and whether the proposed trio captures the decisive contributions.",
        "response": "The decades of routine use establish B030's scientific value, but the distinction from the earlier microscope recognition and the precise attribution remain unresolved. B001 also requires a careful boundary, yet its citation can identify the quantum spin Hall effect directly and separate it from later developments."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the proposed trio captures the decisive experimental realization while representing the group contribution fairly.",
      "How to distinguish the original quantum spin Hall prediction and realization from later confirmations and the wider topological-insulator field."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
